# a-tower-buries-its-own-wall — plan 12 B7, and the phase's finding.
#
# `a-defended-base.keys` plus ONE marker: a tower at (3, 0), one hex
# behind the wall it covers.  Everything else is identical.
#
#   undefended:         61 ticks   ( 41 s)
#   sealed wall:       104 ticks   ( 69 s)
#   this file:          95 ticks   ( 63 s)  — the tower gives back 9
#
# ⚠⚠ **The tower STILL makes the base fall sooner, and it is not a
# bug.**  It is `docs/ENEMY_MOVEMENT.md` § Bodies are terrain arriving
# exactly as written, in a base with nobody to clear up: *"enemies climb
# their own dead onto the wall"*.  The tower kills eight robots at the
# foot of its own wall; three of them fall on (6, 0) and make a 1.5 m
# step; a robot climbs 2.0, and 1.5 + 2.0 clears a 3.0 m wall.  The
# survivors walk over their dead and into the base, and the wall never
# loses a tenth of its HP.
#
# ⚠ **Plan 11 F7b shrank the penalty from ruinous to a rounding.**  On
# the queueing mover this file read 180 ticks against the wall's 311 —
# the tower gave back 131 of the 150 the wall had bought.  With the
# sidestep it gives back 9 of 43.  Both facts have the same cause: the
# body ramp is a fixed prize, and it used to be measured against a clock
# the queue had inflated threefold.  The tower is still a net loss for
# an unattended base, and it is now a small one.
#
# B0 derived that band before anything could die: a single-hex body ramp
# onto a structure `H` high needs a climb of `H / 2`, so onto a `wall`
# the workable pile is [1.0, 2.0] m — *"a couple of dead robots get the
# next one over"*.  This is the first time it has fired in anger, and it
# fires against the player.
#
# ── The ending this one reaches: THE PILE WENT OVER ─────────────
#
# The other of the two `plans/12` asks B7 to distinguish.  The wall is
# INTACT — its middle at 97 of 100 HP and its end still standing with
# 2.7 to spare — and the base fell anyway.  `a-defended-base.keys` is the wall breaking;
# which ending you get is decided by whether you built a tower.
#
# ⚠ **What this does NOT say is that towers are bad.**  It says a tower
# without a cleanup crew is, and the crew is the vehicle plan 12 does
# not build.  Salvage decays, bodies have to be collected at the worst
# possible moment, and `CLAUDE.md` § What dryopea is calls that the
# design's whole test — *does this put something in the player's hands
# at a moment when using it costs them something?*  This file is that
# question with the player removed, and the answer is the base.

echo a tower buries its own wall: eight kills, a body ramp, and a shorter clock

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# The tower, one hex behind the wall.
# ⚠ **ONE hex, and B5b measured why.**  A shot descends from the eye at
# 6.0 m to a robot's body at 1.0 m, so a 3 m wall stops it once it
# stands past ~3/5 of the way to the target: a tower one hex behind its
# wall covers the besiegers, and two hexes back leaves them in dead
# ground.  A tower at the core would see nothing at all.
do cycle_kind
click 3 0
marker 3 0 tower

do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 11 0
marker 11 0 spawn 3

count markers 3 3
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -3 -2 12 -2
drag -3 -1 12 -1
drag -3 0 12 0
drag -3 1 12 1
drag -3 2 12 2
count painted 80 80

palette 9
click 5 -2
click 5 -1
click 5 0
click 5 1
click 5 2
kind 5 0 wall
hp 5 0 100.0 100.0

wave 5
count alive 5 5
wallet 200.0 200.0

tick 22
wave 8

# ⚠ A tower fires one shot a second out of a 30-shot budget, so it is
# spending that budget from the moment the first wave walks into range
# — and every kill is a body nobody will collect.
count alive 9 9

fall 800
ticks 90 100
wallet 0.0 0.0

# ── The ending, stated ──────────────────────────────────────────

# The wall is still there, and all but untouched.  Compare
# `a-defended-base.keys`, where the same wall under the same waves has
# lost its end and a third of its middle by now.
kind 5 0 wall
hp 5 0 95.0 100.0

# ⚠ **And its END was 2.7 HP from going.**  The tower thinned the front
# faster than the front could finish the hex it had nearly broken — so
# this base died with its wall standing and a hex to spare.
kind 5 -2 wall
hp 5 -2 25.0 30.0

# What got them in: three dead robots at the wall's foot.  0.5 m a body
# (`numbers.json` § enemy_regular.body_height), so 1.5 m — inside B0's
# [1.0, 2.0] band, and a 2.0 m climber steps from there onto 3.0 m.
pile 6 0 1.4 1.6

# ⚠ **The tower did its job, and that is the problem.**  Eight of
# thirteen never made it — 24 of its 30 shots, at 3 shots a 30 HP robot
# — and the FIVE survivors took the base anyway, walking in over the
# eight.  A wall and a gun beat by five robots is a stronger statement
# than any clock: the kills are what got them in.
count alive 5 5

snap b7_a_tower_buries_its_own_wall
